  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00038` — **SVG With Embedded Raster**, severity `high`, confidence `0.95`, on `src/assets/partner-logo.svg`.\n\nQuoting the evidence directly:\n\n> \"2 embedded base64 rasters inside the SVG (image/jpeg, image/png)\"\n> - \"2 `<image href=\"data:image/…;base64,…\">` occurrences\"\n> - \"embedded MIME types: image/jpeg, image/png\"\n> - \"SVG byte size: 592 bytes (includes the base64 overhead)\"\n\nSo: **2 embedded rasters**, MIME types **`image/jpeg` and `image/png`**.\n\n## What's actually in the file\n\nA 200×80 viewBox with two 80×80 slots, both of which are `<image>` elements rather than geometry:\n\n| Slot | Line | Position | MIME | Attribute |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | `partner-logo.svg:3` | `x=\"0\"` | `image/png` | `href` |\n| B | `partner-logo.svg:4` | `x=\"100\"` | `image/jpeg` | `xlink:href` |\n\nThere is no `<path>`, `<rect>`, or `<circle>` anywhere — the only non-raster content is `<title>Partner logo</title>`. The file is a raster sandwich wearing an SVG wrapper, which is exactly what the charge says.\n\nTwo things the detector doesn't flag but that matter for the refactor:\n\n- **Slot B uses `xlink:href` with no `xmlns:xlink` declaration on the root element.** Browsers tolerate it under SVG 2, but strict XML parsers and several sanitizers/rasterizers (librsvg, some Inkscape paths, DOMPurify configs) will reject or drop an undeclared namespace prefix — meaning that half of the logo can silently vanish. Fix it in the same pass rather than carrying it forward.\n- **The two payloads in the fixture are 1×1 placeholder pixels** scaled to 80×80. Real byte savings will differ from the 592-byte figure; the structural fix is the same either way.\n\n## Vector vs. standalone raster\n\nThe split follows the codec the author chose, which is a reliable signal of content type:\n\n**Slot A (`image/png`) → re-author as vector paths.** PNG's lossless/indexed encoding is what you pick for flat-colour marks, wordmarks, and hard edges — precisely the content that vectorises cleanly. This is the half that belongs in an SVG.\n\n**Slot B (`image/jpeg`) → split out to a standalone raster.** JPEG is a lossy photographic codec; nobody exports a flat logo mark to JPEG on purpose. It indicates continuous-tone content — a photo, a gradient mesh, a screenshot — which does not vectorise cleanly and would balloon into thousands of path nodes if you tried. Externalise it as pixels.\n\nRecover the originals before deciding anything, in case the fixture is lying about content:\n\n```bash\ncd src/assets\nsed -n '3p' partner-logo.svg | grep -o 'base64,[^\"]*' | cut -d, -f2 | base64 -d > /tmp/slot-a.png\nsed -n '4p' partner-logo.svg | grep -o 'base64,[^\"]*' | cut -d, -f2 | base64 -d > /tmp/slot-b.jpg\nfile /tmp/slot-a.png /tmp/slot-b.jpg\n```\n\n## The constraint that shapes the answer\n\nThe obvious remediation — swap the `data:` URI for `<image href=\"./partner-logo-photo.webp\">` — **does not work if the SVG is consumed via `<img src>` or a CSS `background-image`.** SVG rendered in image context runs in a restricted mode that blocks all external resource loading; the referenced file just never appears. It only works when the SVG is inlined into the DOM or used in an `<object>`/`<iframe>`.\n\nSo the reference target depends on how the logo is consumed. `grep` finds no in-repo importer of `partner-logo.svg` today, so the consumer is external or not yet written — which argues for the option that works everywhere.\n\n**Recommended (works in every consumption mode):** the SVG stops trying to be a composite.\n\n1. Re-export slot A's mark from the design source with the raster layer flattened to paths (Figma → Export SVG with *Outline text*; Illustrator → Object ▸ Image Trace ▸ Expand for a traced source). Paste the resulting `<path>`/`<g>` into `partner-logo.svg` under `<g transform=\"translate(0,0)\">`.\n2. Write slot B out as `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp`, resized to **160×160** (2× the 80×80 render box, for HiDPI) and re-encoded — WebP at q80, with a `.jpg` sibling only if you need pre-2020 browser support.\n3. Delete both `<image>` elements from the SVG, and remove the now-unused `xlink` reference along with them.\n4. Tighten `viewBox` to `0 0 80 80` (and `width`/`height` to match) since the SVG now carries only the left mark — or keep `0 0 200 80` if the empty right half is intentional whitespace.\n5. Compose the two at the consumer: a `<span class=\"partner-logo\">` holding `<img src=\"partner-logo.svg\">` and `<img src=\"partner-logo-photo.webp\">` side by side, or the SVG as CSS `background-image` with the photo as an adjacent element.\n\n**Alternative, only if the single-file composite is a hard requirement:** keep both slots in the SVG, but replace the JPEG `data:` URI with `<image href=\"./partner-logo-photo.webp\" x=\"100\" y=\"0\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\"/>` and commit to **inlining** the SVG in the DOM at every call site. Document that constraint in a comment inside the file, because the next person to write `<img src=\"partner-logo.svg\">` will ship a half-blank logo with no error anywhere.\n\n## Files after the refactor\n\n- `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` — **kept at the same path**, same filename, vector geometry only. No `data:` URIs, no `<image>`, no `xlink`. Nothing that references the logo has to change.\n- `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.webp` — **new**, 160×160, the externalised photographic half.\n- `src/assets/partner-logo-photo.jpg` — optional legacy fallback, only if your browser support matrix needs it.\n- Whichever component or stylesheet composes the two, if you take the recommended path.\n\n## Verification\n\n```bash\ngrep -c 'data:image' src/assets/partner-logo.svg   # expect 0\ngrep -c 'xlink' src/assets/partner-logo.svg        # expect 0\ncrimes scan --format json | jq '.findings[] | select(.type==\"svg_with_embedded_raster\")'\n```\n\nThe last command should return nothing. Confirm the rendered logo still matches at 1×, 2×, and at a large scale — the whole point of the change is that the vector half now stays crisp where the embedded PNG could not.\n\nTwo neighbouring asset findings are **out of scope** for this refactor but sit in the same directory: `crime_00040` (`check-icon.png`, 32×32, should be an SVG icon) and `crime_00041` (`hero-banner.png`, 253.9 KB, wants a resize plus WebP/AVIF re-encode). Worth batching into one asset-hygiene pass if you're already in the design tool.",
